WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.308
Documentation is part of the toolkit because DFIR work must be explainable.

00:00:05.308 --> 00:00:11.795
Case notes should capture actions taken, observations, timestamps, decisions, evidence references, assumptions, and open questions.

00:00:11.795 --> 00:00:20.247
Notes help analysts keep their own thinking organized, but they also help other responders understand what has already been done.

00:00:20.247 --> 00:00:28.700
During a long incident, good notes prevent repeated work and reduce the risk of losing important context during shift changes.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:40.298
An evidence inventory should identify each item collected, where it came from, who collected it, when it was collected, where it is stored, and how integrity was checked.

00:00:40.298 --> 00:00:46.392
Chain of custody forms extend that record by documenting transfers, handling, access, and storage.

00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:52.093
These records matter even when an incident does not become a legal case.

00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:57.794
They support confidence in the findings and help the organization defend its process.

00:00:57.794 --> 00:01:00.349
Timeline templates are also valuable.

00:01:00.349 --> 00:01:05.264
A useful timeline separates observed facts from analysis and response actions.

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:14.896
For example, it should distinguish a suspicious login, an administrator disabling an account, an analyst exporting logs, and a business owner approving recovery.

00:01:14.896 --> 00:01:24.921
Decision logs capture why key actions were taken, such as isolating a system, delaying a reboot, notifying leadership, or prioritizing one service over another.

00:01:24.921 --> 00:01:29.050
Reporting templates help turn investigation work into usable communication.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:35.143
A technical appendix may support analysts, while an executive summary may support leadership decisions.

00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:39.272
Screenshots should be labeled and tied to evidence references.

00:01:39.272 --> 00:01:49.690
Scripts and procedures should live in version control so the team can track changes, review updates, and know which version was used during a case.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:56.570
Documentation is not paperwork for its own sake; it is how DFIR becomes repeatable and defensible.
